---
import Layout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Research Framework | Rex Autistikōn Labs">
  <main class="rf-page">
    <section class="hero">
      <p class="kicker">Research Framework</p>
      <h1>From research question to falsifiable test</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">
        Rex Autistikōn Labs operates an independent research program on whether chronic mechanical
        restriction in defined sensor-rich myofascial and visceral zones contributes to altered
        afferent signaling relevant to sensory and interoceptive phenotypes. This page separates
        established science, proposed mechanism, predictions, and experimental path.
      </p>
    </section>

    <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <a href="#question">1. Research question</a>
      <a href="#hypothesis">2. Hypothesis</a>
      <a href="#evidence">3. Evidence matrix</a>
      <a href="#zones">4. Zone taxonomy</a>
      <a href="#model">5. Computational model</a>
      <a href="#testing">6. How we test it</a>
      <a href="#falsify">7. Falsifiable predictions</a>
      <a href="#limits">8. Scope and limits</a>
      <a href="#platform">9. Research platform</a>
    </nav>

    <!-- 1 -->
    <section id="question" class="block">
      <h2>1. Research question</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        Why do differences in sensory processing, proprioception, interoception, and related
        motor–autonomic features often co-occur in some neurodevelopmental presentations, and
        can part of that covariance be linked to measurable mechanical conditions in specific
        peripheral interfaces?
      </p>
      <p>
        Conventional accounts emphasize central neural computation, genetics, and developmental
        difference. Those accounts are necessary but may be incomplete if peripheral
        mechanoreceptive and interoceptive interfaces systematically bias the afferent sample
        available to central systems. The question is not whether the brain matters; it is
        whether defined peripheral mechanical states are a tractable, measurable contributor
        to afferent statistics.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 2 -->
    <section id="hypothesis" class="block">
      <h2>2. Hypothesis</h2>
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <strong>Working hypothesis.</strong> Chronic mechanical restriction within specific
          anatomical zones densely invested with mechanoreceptors and interoceptors can distort
          afferent signaling (amplitude, timing, and effective precision of ascending input).
          Over time, such distortion may contribute to sustained differences in sensory,
          proprioceptive, and interoceptive regulation. The effect is proposed to be
          zone-specific, cumulative, and quantifiable from tissue-level parameters.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p>
        This is a <em>mechanistic research hypothesis</em>, not a claim that autism or any
        neurodevelopmental condition is caused by fascia, reduced to peripheral tissue, or
        cured by mechanical intervention. Neurodevelopmental phenotypes are heterogeneous;
        peripheral mechanics, if relevant, would be one contributor among many.
      </p>
      <h3>Logical chain (explicit)</h3>
      <ol class="chain">
        <li><span>Established</span> Mechanoreceptors and interoceptors transduce tissue deformation and internal state into afferent signals.</li>
        <li><span>Established / emerging</span> Fascia and soft tissue exhibit viscoelastic behavior; mechanical context can modulate receptor signaling.</li>
        <li><span>RAL hypothesis</span> Zone-level restriction (operationalized as parameter <em>r</em> with stiffness <em>G</em> and viscosity <em>η</em>) systematically reduces effective transmitted afferent amplitude and raises prediction-error / free-energy style costs in a multi-zone model.</li>
        <li><span>RAL prediction</span> Structured differences in zone parameters will produce reproducible metric patterns (fidelity, π<sub>eff</sub>, free energy, MPA summaries) that can be compared across sessions, groups, and regions without implying diagnosis.</li>
        <li><span>Open</span> Whether those metric patterns track sensory/interoceptive behavioral measures is an empirical question for controlled studies.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <!-- 3 -->
    <section id="evidence" class="block">
      <h2>3. Evidence matrix</h2>
      <p>
        The matrix below separates what is broadly established from what RAL treats as hypothesis.
        Strength labels are qualitative program judgments for communication, not meta-analytic grades.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Component</th>
              <th>Status</th>
              <th>Evidence strength</th>
              <th>Role in RAL program</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Proprioceptive signaling from muscle and joint receptors</td>
              <td><span class="tag est">Established</span></td>
              <td>High</td>
              <td>Background science</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Interoceptive pathways and body-state representation</td>
              <td><span class="tag est">Established</span></td>
              <td>High</td>
              <td>Background science</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Viscoelastic behavior of soft tissue (Kelvin–Voigt-type models)</td>
              <td><span class="tag est">Established</span></td>
              <td>High</td>
              <td>Modeling substrate</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Fascial and soft-tissue mechanotransduction</td>
              <td><span class="tag em">Emerging</span></td>
              <td>Moderate</td>
              <td>Motivates zone focus</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Sensor-rich investment of selected cranio-cervical, oral, pharyngeal, visceral, and pelvic interfaces</td>
              <td><span class="tag em">Emerging</span></td>
              <td>Moderate (anatomy-dependent)</td>
              <td>Zone taxonomy</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Chronic mechanical restriction → systematically altered afference at these zones</td>
              <td><span class="tag hyp">RAL hypothesis</span></td>
              <td>Limited / emerging</td>
              <td>Core claim under test</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Altered afference → neurodevelopmental phenotype</td>
              <td><span class="tag open">Unestablished</span></td>
              <td>Unknown</td>
              <td>Long-range question; not assumed</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Operational 8-zone metric pipeline (fidelity, π<sub>eff</sub>, free energy, MPA)</td>
              <td><span class="tag hyp">RAL construct</span></td>
              <td>Model-defined</td>
              <td>Measurement layer for tests</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="note">
        Citation expansion for each row is a living task of the program. Where peer-reviewed
        support is strong, it will be linked from zone dossiers and publications; where support
        is weak, language remains provisional.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 4 -->
    <section id="zones" class="block">
      <h2>4. Zone taxonomy (eight sensor-rich interfaces)</h2>
      <p>
        Intake, calculation, and results use one consistent eight-zone schema. Labels denote
        research interfaces, not diagnostic categories.
      </p>
      <div class="zone-grid">
        <div class="zone-card"><span class="zn">1</span><div><strong>Extraocular / Orbital</strong><p>Oculomotor–orbital soft tissue and periocular mechanical context.</p></div></div>
        <div class="zone-card"><span class="zn">2</span><div><strong>Middle-ear</strong><p>Middle-ear mechanical environment relevant to auditory coupling.</p></div></div>
        <div class="zone-card"><span class="zn">3</span><div><strong>Nasal</strong><p>Nasal airway and associated soft-tissue mechanical context.</p></div></div>
        <div class="zone-card"><span class="zn">4</span><div><strong>Tongue</strong><p>Tongue and floor-of-mouth complex.</p></div></div>
        <div class="zone-card"><span class="zn">5</span><div><strong>Pharyngeal / Laryngeal</strong><p>Pharyngeal–laryngeal soft tissue interfaces.</p></div></div>
        <div class="zone-card"><span class="zn">6</span><div><strong>Suboccipital</strong><p>Suboccipital muscular–fascial complex and upper cervical mechanical context.</p></div></div>
        <div class="zone-card"><span class="zn">7</span><div><strong>Visceral fascia / Diaphragm</strong><p>Diaphragmatic and visceral fascial mechanical interface.</p></div></div>
        <div class="zone-card"><span class="zn">8</span><div><strong>Pelvic floor</strong><p>Pelvic floor and perineal mechanical interface.</p></div></div>
      </div>
      <p class="note">
        Anatomical detail, candidate receptors, and literature for each zone are intended to
        live as zone dossiers in the interactive model (evidence status marked explicitly).
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 5 -->
    <section id="model" class="block">
      <h2>5. Computational model (operational layer)</h2>
      <p>
        For each zone, collaborators supply restriction <em>r</em>, stiffness <em>G</em>,
        viscosity <em>η</em>, and an integer MPA score. A Kelvin–Voigt-inspired transmission
        step yields a transmitted amplitude treated as fidelity; an effective precision-weight
        proxy π<sub>eff</sub> and a free-energy-style term <em>F</em> are computed so sessions
        can be compared on a common scale.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>What the model is:</strong> a transparent, session-level scoring layer for multi-zone mechanical parameters.</li>
        <li><strong>What the model is not:</strong> a validated biomarker, a diagnostic classifier, or proof of central pathophysiology.</li>
        <li><strong>Why it exists:</strong> without shared metrics, the hypothesis cannot be tested across labs in a comparable way.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Model versioning is recorded on calculation outputs. Refinement of constitutive
        assumptions (linearity, frequency dependence, zone coupling) is an expected part of
        the program, not a retrospective embarrassment.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 6 -->
    <section id="testing" class="block">
      <h2>6. How we test it (experimental program)</h2>
      <p>
        The sequence is deliberately ordered so later phases depend on earlier measurement
        competence. Phases may overlap once ethics and methods are in place.
      </p>
      <div class="phases">
        <div class="phase">
          <h3>Phase I — Schema and anatomical mapping</h3>
          <p>
            Stabilize zone definitions, parameter ranges, and documentation. Align anatomical
            claims with literature; mark weak claims as provisional. Deliverable: locked
            taxonomy + intake schema (in operation).
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="phase">
          <h3>Phase II — Physiological and mechanical measurement</h3>
          <p>
            Pair zone parameters with independent measures where available (e.g. elastography-like
            stiffness estimates, structured clinical observation protocols, sensory threshold
            batteries chosen by partner labs). Deliverable: paired datasets under partner IRB.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="phase">
          <h3>Phase III — Controlled mechanical perturbation</h3>
          <p>
            Test whether specified, ethical mechanical changes at a zone produce predicted
            short-horizon shifts in RAL metrics and in partner-chosen physiological readouts.
            Deliverable: preregistered perturbation experiments.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="phase">
          <h3>Phase IV — Correlation with sensory / interoceptive measures</h3>
          <p>
            Assess whether metric patterns relate to validated sensory or interoceptive
            instruments—without treating correlation as diagnosis. Deliverable: associative
            analyses with transparent multiple-comparison practice.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="phase">
          <h3>Phase V — Intervention-linked studies (only if warranted)</h3>
          <p>
            Only if earlier phases support mechanistic relevance, test whether targeted
            protocols change metrics and agreed outcomes. Deliverable: intervention trials
            designed to fail clearly if effects are absent.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="note">
        Partner institutions remain responsible for human-subjects compliance. RAL provides
        governed intake, calculation, storage, and de-identified aggregate views.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 7 -->
    <section id="falsify" class="block">
      <h2>7. Falsifiable predictions</h2>
      <p>
        The program is committed to revision or rejection when predictions fail under adequate tests.
      </p>
      <div class="pred-list">
        <div class="pred">
          <h3>P1 — Metric sensitivity</h3>
          <p>
            If restriction <em>r</em> is increased in a single zone while <em>G</em>, <em>η</em>,
            and global parameters are held fixed within the model, computed fidelity for that
            zone should decrease and free-energy-style cost should non-decrease. Failure of this
            internal relationship under the published equations requires model correction.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="pred">
          <h3>P2 — Session reliability</h3>
          <p>
            Under standardized acquisition, repeated sessions on stable subjects should show
            higher within-subject metric reliability than between randomly paired subjects
            (thresholds preregistered per study). Persistent pure noise implies the operational
            parameters are not capturing a stable latent state.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="pred">
          <h3>P3 — Perturbation coupling</h3>
          <p>
            If Phase III perturbations are correctly targeted, predicted zones should change
            more than pre-specified control zones. Global undifferentiated change, or change
            only in control zones, weakens the zone-specific hypothesis.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="pred">
          <h3>P4 — External correlate (conditional)</h3>
          <p>
            Where partners collect validated sensory/interoceptive measures, preregistered
            associations with RAL metrics should replicate in direction. Repeated null results
            under adequate power require narrowing or abandoning phenotype-linking claims—
            without automatically voiding the biomechanical scoring layer itself.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="pred">
          <h3>P5 — Collective structure</h3>
          <p>
            De-identified group-level aggregates should not invent stable “signatures” that
            disappear under split-half or multi-lab replication. Non-replicable aggregate
            stories will be withdrawn from public scientific claims.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 8 -->
    <section id="limits" class="block">
      <h2>8. Scope and limits</h2>
      <div class="limits">
        <div>
          <h3>We claim</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>A clear research question and mechanistic hypothesis.</li>
            <li>An operational multi-zone parameter schema and calculation pipeline.</li>
            <li>Governed collaboration, authentication, and de-identified aggregation tools.</li>
            <li>Willingness to revise or reject predictions under test.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>We do not claim</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>That autism or any condition is caused by myofascial restriction alone.</li>
            <li>Diagnostic accuracy or clinical triage use.</li>
            <li>Proven therapeutic efficacy of any intervention.</li>
            <li>Institutional endorsement by universities or clinics listed as collaborators unless explicitly stated by them.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        Independent status is intentional: Rex Autistikon Research Foundation, Inc. is a
        501(c)(3) public charity (EIN 42-3220323). Determination documentation is available
        via the Transparency page or upon request.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 9 -->
    <section id="platform" class="block">
      <h2>9. Research platform</h2>
      <p>
        The public program is paired with a live technical platform so tests are not purely rhetorical:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/collaborate">Collaborator access request</a> — reviewed institutional onboarding.</li>
        <li><a href="/intake">Data Intake</a> — eight-zone session submission.</li>
        <li><a href="/results">Results Dashboard</a> — personal history and de-identified collective laboratory views.</li>
        <li><a href="/model">Interactive model</a> — conceptual exploration of interfaces (dossier depth expanding).</li>
        <li><a href="/publications">Publications</a> — primary documents, DOI, and future methods/data releases.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="closing">
        A skeptical reader should be able to disagree with the hypothesis and still recognize
        a structured attempt to state assumptions, measurements, and failure conditions.
        That is the standard this framework is written to meet.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Layout>

<style>
  .rf-page { background: #f8fafc; color: #0f172a; }
  .hero {
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, #0f766e 0%, #134e4a 100%);
    color: #fff; padding: 3rem 1.25rem 2.5rem; text-align: center;
  }
  .kicker {
    text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.08em; font-size: 0.75rem;
    opacity: 0.85; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; font-weight: 600;
  }
  .hero h1 {
    font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 3vw, 2.25rem); font-weight: 700;
    max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto 0.75rem; line-height: 1.25;
  }
  .subtitle {
    max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto; line-height: 1.6; opacity: 0.92; font-size: 1.02rem;
  }

  .toc {
    max-width: 900px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 1.25rem 1.25rem 0;
    display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.5rem 0.85rem; justify-content: center;
  }
  .toc a {
    font-size: 0.8rem; color: #0f766e; text-decoration: none; font-weight: 600;
  }
  .toc a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }

  .block {
    max-width: 900px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 2.25rem 1.25rem 0.5rem;
  }
  .block h2 {
    font-size: 1.35rem; margin: 0 0 0.85rem; color: #0f172a;
    padding-bottom: 0.4rem; border-bottom: 2px solid #ccfbf1;
  }
  .block h3 { font-size: 1.02rem; margin: 1.25rem 0 0.5rem; color: #134e4a; }
  .block p, .block li { line-height: 1.65; color: #334155; }
  .lead { font-size: 1.08rem; color: #1e293b; }

  .callout {
    background: #fff; border: 1px solid #99f6e4; border-left: 4px solid #0f766e;
    border-radius: 10px; padding: 1rem 1.15rem; margin: 0.75rem 0 1rem;
  }
  .callout p { margin: 0; color: #0f172a; }

  .chain { margin: 0.75rem 0 0; padding-left: 1.1rem; }
  .chain li { margin-bottom: 0.65rem; }
  .chain span {
    display: inline-block; font-size: 0.68rem; font-weight: 700; text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em; background: #ecfdf5; color: #065f46;
    padding: 0.15rem 0.45rem; border-radius: 4px; margin-right: 0.35rem;
  }

  .table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; margin: 1rem 0; border-radius: 10px; border: 1px solid #e2e8f0; }
  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; background: #fff; font-size: 0.88rem; }
  th, td { padding: 0.7rem 0.75rem; text-align: left; border-bottom: 1px solid #e2e8f0; vertical-align: top; }
  th { background: #f1f5f9; color: #475569; font-size: 0.75rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.03em; }
  .tag {
    display: inline-block; font-size: 0.68rem; font-weight: 700; text-transform: uppercase;
    padding: 0.15rem 0.4rem; border-radius: 4px; letter-spacing: 0.03em;
  }
  .tag.est { background: #d1fae5; color: #065f46; }
  .tag.em { background: #e0e7ff; color: #3730a3; }
  .tag.hyp { background: #fef3c7; color: #92400e; }
  .tag.open { background: #f1f5f9; color: #475569; }

  .zone-grid {
